LESSONS_S6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Taxes and invoicing preparation: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the sales tax machinery, pricing and discount governance, and the preparation the Sales Invoice depends on. On completing this module you will be able to: state the Nigerian VAT context a selling configuration operates in; build and read Sales Taxes and Charges Templates; operate item tax templates on mixed documents; apply the inclusive and exclusive pricing postures correctly; govern the three discount instruments; and run the weekly watching reports.</p><p><b>Position on the document timeline.</b> The selling documents post their effects in a fixed sequence: the Sales Order reserves stock (module 4), the Delivery Note deducts stock and posts cost (module 5), and the Sales Invoice posts revenue, tax, and the receivable (module 7). This module prepares the third step: the tax computation, the pricing integrity, and the reads that confirm both, so that invoicing in module 7 is an execution task rather than a correction task. Each preparation area below has its own chapter, its own custody assignment, and its own confirming read on a stated cadence.</p><p><b>What must be true before invoicing.</b> The pre-invoicing state this module establishes:</p><p>1. <b>The tax templates exist and are selected correctly</b> — the document-level template (Chapter 3) and the item-level treatments (Chapter 4) together compute the tax the return will declare.<br>2. <b>The pricing posture is consistent</b> — inclusive or exclusive per channel by policy (Chapter 5), matching what was quoted (module 3's rule that the offer's tax total is the invoice's tax total).<br>3. <b>Discounts carry their approvals</b> — every departure from list price is one of the three governed instruments (Chapter 6).<br>4. <b>The delivered quantities are known</b> — module 5's %delivered figures state what is billable per the payment schedule.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The accountant owns the tax configuration and rulings — templates, account heads, item classifications, and the return itself; the sales function selects the decided templates and operates within them; and the standing rule for the module: <b>tax on a document is computed by configuration, never edited by hand.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: VAT in Nigeria. Chapter 3: Sales Taxes and Charges Templates. Chapter 4: item tax templates on documents. Chapter 5: inclusive and exclusive pricing. Chapter 6: discounts governance. Chapter 7: the watching reports. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — VAT in Nigeria", "12", "<p>The tax the selling configuration computes most often is Value Added Tax. This chapter states the context.</p><p><b>1. Output VAT.</b> Nigerian VAT stands at <b>7.5%</b>. When the business sells taxable goods or services, it charges VAT on the sale — added on top of the net price (exclusive) or contained within the shelf price (inclusive; Chapter 5 covers both arithmetics). The amount charged is not the business's revenue: it is collected on behalf of the government, held as a liability, and remitted with the periodic VAT return.</p><p><b>2. The liability flow.</b> The accounting path every charged naira of VAT follows:</p><p>1. <b>At invoicing</b> — the tax row posts to the VAT output liability account (the account head the template names; Chapter 3).<br>2. <b>Through the period</b> — the liability account accumulates the VAT charged on all sales.<br>3. <b>At the return</b> — the accountant computes the period's position (output VAT less recoverable input VAT from the purchasing side, per the Procurement and Accounts tracks) and remits per the filing calendar.</p><p>The selling function's obligation in this flow is accuracy at step 1: the correct rate, on the correct base, posted to the correct account, on every document — because the return declares what the documents computed.</p><p><b>3. Exempt and zero-rated classes.</b> Not all goods carry the standard rate. Nigerian VAT legislation exempts defined classes — including many pharmaceutical products, the class most relevant to the standing case — and zero-rates others. The operational consequences:</p><p>1. <b>The item's treatment is stated on the item side</b> (module 2, Chapter 7): the classification per the accountant's ruling table, applied at the item gate.<br>2. <b>Mixed documents compute per line</b> (Chapter 4): exempt rows at their treatment, standard rows at 7.5%, on one invoice.<br>3. <b>Treatment changes are the accountant's dated rulings</b>, never operator edits — the audit trail of a tax classification is examined in every tax audit.</p><p><b>4. The e-invoicing context.</b> Where the business operates under the FIRS e-invoicing regime, submitted Sales Invoices transmit to the tax authority through the site's e-invoicing integration per the compliance configuration. The operational rules this adds: invoices carry the customer's TIN (module 1's gate requirement), the item and tax data must be complete and correct at submission because the transmitted document is the declared document, and corrections follow the credit-note lane (module 7) rather than deletion — the transmitted record is permanent.</p><p><b>5. The boundary.</b> The accountant rules on taxability, rates, and the return; this module's chapters equip the selling function to configure and operate what the accountant has ruled. A tax question arising on a document — an unfamiliar class, a customer claiming exemption — routes to the accountant and waits for the ruling, per the standing rule from module 2.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Sales Taxes and Charges Templates", "12", "<p>The document-level tax computation is configured as a template. This chapter is the doctype and its maintenance.</p><p><b>1. The template record.</b> Open Sales Taxes and Charges Template (awesome bar) &gt; New: a named, ordered set of rows, each computing one charge. Documents select a template; the template's rows compute the document's tax section.</p><p><b>2. Row anatomy.</b> Each row carries:</p><p>1. <b>Type</b> — the computation base:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;— <b>On Net Total</b>: a percentage of the document's net total — the standard base for VAT.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;— <b>Actual</b>: a fixed amount — used for flat charges such as a delivery fee.<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;— <b>On Previous Row Amount / On Previous Row Total</b>: a percentage computed on an earlier row's result — used where a charge compounds on another charge; rare in the standing configuration and used only by the accountant's design.<br>2. <b>Account Head</b> — the ledger account the row posts to: the VAT output liability for the VAT row; the freight recovery account for a delivery charge. The account head is the connection between the document and the return, and it is the accountant's field.<br>3. <b>Rate or Amount</b> — 7.5 for the standard VAT row; the fixed figure for an Actual row.<br>4. <b>Description</b> — the text that prints on the document's tax section.</p><p><b>3. The standing template set.</b> Per the site's design:</p><p>1. <b>The standard VAT template</b> — one row: VAT, On Net Total, 7.5%, posting to the VAT output liability. Set as the default so documents carry it unless a decided exception applies.<br>2. <b>Variants only by decision</b> — a delivery-charge variant (the VAT row plus an Actual freight row) for routes that bill freight; an export or zero-rated template where the business has such trade, per the accountant's ruling. Each variant is a register entry with its purpose; template proliferation is avoided for the same reason as price-list proliferation — every template is configuration to maintain and an opportunity to select wrongly.</p><p><b>4. Maintenance.</b> Templates are created and changed by the accountant (or by the configuration role at the accountant's written instruction): a statutory rate change, a new account head, a new variant — each a governed, dated change. Template-write permission is held per the matrix; the selling function selects templates and reads their results, and does not edit their rows.</p><p><b>5. Reading a document's tax section.</b> The verification habit: the tax section of any document shows the template's rows with their computed amounts. The reader confirms: the expected template name, the rate against the base (7.5% of the net total the rows show), and the total consistent with the pricing posture (Chapter 5). A tax section that cannot be read back to its template and rate is a configuration question to resolve before the document proceeds.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Item tax templates on documents", "12", "<p>Module 2, Chapter 7 set each item's tax classification. This chapter is the document-side arithmetic: how mixed taxability computes on one invoice.</p><p><b>1. The override mechanics.</b> An Item Tax Template attaches to an item (or derives from its item group) and overrides the document template's rate <b>for that row only</b>:</p><p>1. The document template states VAT 7.5% On Net Total.<br>2. The exempt item's template states VAT 0% for the same account head.<br>3. On the document, the standard rows compute at 7.5%; the exempt rows compute at 0%; the tax section totals the per-line results.</p><p>The design means one document serves any basket: the pharmacy invoice carrying exempt medicines and standard-rated consumables computes each class correctly without splitting the sale across documents or editing any rate by hand.</p><p><b>2. What the operator sees and verifies.</b> On a mixed document:</p><p>1. <b>Per-line tax detail</b> — the item-wise tax breakup (the document's tax detail view) shows each row's rate and amount: the exempt rows at zero, the standard rows at 7.5%.<br>2. <b>The verification read</b> — for any unfamiliar mix, the reader confirms a sample line of each class against the ruling table: the exempt item computing at zero because its template says so, not because someone edited the row.<br>3. <b>The refusal rule</b> — a line computing at an unexpected rate is a classification question (the item's template wrong or missing), resolved at the item record through the accountant's lane; the document is not corrected by hand because the next document would repeat the error.</p><p><b>3. The interaction with the document template.</b> The two levels divide the work: the document template states the context's charges (VAT, and any freight or context charges); the item template states each product's treatment within that context. Selecting a different document template does not change an item's classification, and an item's classification applies on every document template that carries the account head. The division means each fact is stated once — the context on the document, the treatment on the item — per the standing one-source pattern.</p><p><b>4. The classification read, restated.</b> The quarterly item tax read (module 2, Chapter 7) is the register-side control; this chapter adds the document-side sample: a monthly draw of mixed invoices checked line-by-line against the ruling table, riding the accountant's period close. Together the two reads confirm that what the register states and what the documents compute agree — which is the condition the VAT return depends on.</p><p><b>5. Zero-rated versus exempt, operationally.</b> The accountant's distinction (zero-rated sales are taxable at 0% with input recovery; exempt sales are outside the charge with input restrictions) determines the return's treatment, not the document operator's procedure: both classes reach the document as item templates computing zero on the line. The operator's obligation is the same in both cases — the correct template on the item, verified, never hand-edited — and the return-side difference is the accountant's territory.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Inclusive and exclusive pricing", "12", "<p>One setting separates two pricing arithmetics. This chapter states both and the policy that governs them.</p><p><b>1. Exclusive pricing (the default).</b> The entered rate is the net price; tax adds on top:</p><p>1. Rate ₦1,000, VAT 7.5% → tax ₦75, total <b>₦1,075</b>.<br>2. The document shows net ₦1,000, VAT ₦75, gross ₦1,075.<br>3. This is the business-to-business posture: trade customers, procurement offices, and input-credit accounting work in net prices, and the Trade price list carries net rates.</p><p><b>2. Inclusive pricing.</b> The entered rate already contains the tax; the system extracts it:</p><p>1. Rate ₦1,075 inclusive, VAT 7.5% → net ₦1,000, tax ₦75, total ₦1,075.<br>2. The extraction divides by (1 + rate): ₦1,075 ÷ 1.075 = ₦1,000 net.<br>3. This is the retail posture: shelf and counter prices are the prices customers pay, and the retail price list carries inclusive rates with the inclusive setting on the tax row.</p><p><b>3. The policy rule.</b> The posture is set per channel by written policy — the retail list inclusive, the trade list exclusive — configured once in the price lists and templates, and never mixed ad hoc:</p><p>1. <b>Confusing the postures misstates either the price or the VAT.</b> An inclusive rate entered where the system expects exclusive charges the customer 7.5% over the intended price; an exclusive rate entered as inclusive under-declares the VAT by the same proportion.<br>2. <b>The quotation-to-invoice consistency rule</b> (module 3) depends on the posture holding constant through the document chain: what was quoted at a posture invoices at that posture.<br>3. <b>Posture changes are configuration decisions</b> — a channel moving from one posture to the other is a governed change with the price lists re-based arithmetically and the change dated, because every price on the list means something different after the switch.</p><p><b>4. Rounding.</b> The document computes line and total rounding per the site's rounding configuration; the rounded figures are the document's figures. The operational rule: rounding differences are configuration matters for the accountant, not amounts to be adjusted by editing a tax row — a tax section is never hand-balanced.</p><p><b>5. Reading a priced document.</b> The check that closes the chapter: given any document, the reader states its posture (from the list and template), confirms the arithmetic in the correct direction (net plus tax, or gross containing tax), and confirms the totals match the customer's expectation for the channel. The three-line check takes under a minute and precedes any conversation about a total a customer disputes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Discounts governance", "12", "<p>Three instruments reduce a price below list. This chapter assigns each its holder and its record, consolidating the controls the track has built.</p><p><b>1. The three instruments and their holders.</b></p><p>1. <b>The price list</b> (module 2) — the standing class price, maintained by the pricing function through the governed update. A class that always pays less has a list (or a list-derived arrangement), not a permanent discount entered per document.<br>2. <b>The Pricing Rule</b> (module 2, Chapter 6) — the standing condition: quantity breaks, promotions, class discounts — registered, owned, dated, firing by configuration. Policy that repeats belongs here, so that it applies uniformly and expires on its date.<br>3. <b>The document-level concession</b> — the row rate edit, the row discount percentage, and the additional discount field on the document total: the negotiator's instruments for genuine one-case concessions, governed by the deviation ladder (module 3) at quotation time and by the amendment taxonomy (module 4) after commitment.</p><p><b>2. The assignment rule.</b> Each price departure uses the instrument that matches its nature: standing class pricing in lists; repeating conditions in rules; one-case concessions on documents with their approvals. Misassignment is the governance failure the reads detect — the repeating document discount that should be a rule (the same customer conceded the same percentage monthly by hand), or the rule created for one negotiation that should have been a document concession with an approval.</p><p><b>3. The additional discount field.</b> The document-total discount (percentage or amount, applied on net or gross per configuration) is the broadest instrument and carries the tightest governance: permitted roles per the matrix, the ladder band by size, the reason on the document, and visibility in the discounts read. Its legitimate uses are whole-document commercial decisions (the negotiated package discount); its illegitimate use is as a convenience field that hides per-line pricing from the record.</p><p><b>4. Visibility.</b> Every instrument leaves its price visible against list:</p><p>1. The document shows the price list rate and the transacted rate per row; the difference is the concession, readable per line.<br>2. Rule effects are attributable — the applied rule is identifiable on the row's pricing detail.<br>3. The discounts read (below) aggregates the differences.</p><p><b>5. The discounts read — monthly.</b> The month's documents with transacted rates below list, grouped by instrument, customer, and approver: list-versus-transacted value by customer group (the standing concession pattern that should become a rule or a list), document concessions against their ladder approvals (the module 3 read continued through orders and invoices), and additional-discount usage by role. The read files with the pricing review, and its output is the owner's data for recalibrating lists, rules, and bands.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The watching reports", "12", "<p>Configuration is confirmed by reading. This chapter is the weekly reporting habit and the reads it comprises.</p><p><b>1. Sales Analytics — the movement view.</b> Revenue cut by customer, item, item group, customer group, or territory, across time periods — the dimensions the track's registers disciplined (modules 1-2) are the dimensions this report can cut by. The standing read is the <b>trend read</b>: the current period against the prior periods on each major dimension. A movement of the order of <b>30% across three periods</b> on any dimension — a customer's volume, a group's revenue, a territory's mix — is a change to investigate and explain, not a figure to note: the explanation (a gained or lost account, a price change, a stock gap, a seasonal pattern) is written into the week's note so the following reader inherits it.</p><p><b>2. The sales register — the tax view.</b> The period's invoices with their taxable values and tax amounts by rate class: the figures the VAT return will declare. The weekly pass confirms the period is accumulating correctly — standard-rated value at 7.5%, exempt value at zero, the split consistent with the business's mix — so the return at period end is a compilation, not an investigation. Anomalies (an exempt-class item accumulating standard-rated tax) route to the classification lane at the item record.</p><p><b>3. The execution reads, consolidated.</b> The track's weekly reads meet here as one screen sequence: open quotations by validity (module 3), overdue and at-risk order rows (module 4), delivered-not-billed (module 5), pending acknowledgments (module 5), and the discounts summary (Chapter 6). Each has its own procedure in its own module; the habit this chapter adds is the consolidated pass.</p><p><b>4. The fifteen-minute five-check read — weekly.</b> The consolidated pass, as a fixed procedure:</p><p>1. <b>Open quotations</b> — expiring offers followed up; expired ones closed.<br>2. <b>Overdue order rows</b> — each with owner and action.<br>3. <b>Delivered-not-billed</b> — the list that must clear at the invoicing run.<br>4. <b>The discounts summary</b> — concessions against approvals.<br>5. <b>The trend read</b> — the analytics pass with its written explanations.</p><p>The five checks take approximately <b>fifteen minutes</b> on a maintained book, and the output is a short written note: the exceptions, their owners, their actions. The note files with the sales records; the month's four notes are the material of the monthly commercial review.</p><p><b>5. The monthly commercial review.</b> The owner's session, fed by the weekly notes and the monthly reads (conversion, deviations, discounts, returns): the book's state, the pipeline's state, the pricing controls' evidence, and the decisions — list updates, rule changes, band recalibrations — each entering the governed lanes the track has defined. The review closes the loop this module opened: configuration, operation, reading, adjustment.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade's tax and pricing layer", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to the standing case. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the VAT spine (Chapters 2-3).</b> One standard template: <b>VAT, On Net Total, 7.5%</b>, posting to the VAT output liability account the accountant confirmed; set as the default template. One variant by decision: the delivery-charge template — the VAT row plus an Actual freight row posting to freight recovery — for the routes that bill freight. Both entered in the configuration register with their purposes; template-write permission confirmed as the accountant's role only.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the item treatments (Chapter 4).</b> The ruling table applies by class: the exempt pharmaceutical classes carry the 0% item tax template; consumables and equipment carry standard treatment. The mixed-basket verification draft — exempt medicines and standard consumables on one document — computes per line: exempt rows at zero, standard rows at 7.5%, the item-wise breakup readable. The draft is deleted after reading; the sample files with the quarter's classification read.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the postures (Chapter 5).</b> By written policy: the retail channel prices <b>inclusive</b> — counter prices are what customers pay, the Standard Selling list carries inclusive rates, and the inclusive setting is on; the trade channel prices <b>exclusive</b> — the Trade list carries net rates, and Montaigne AH's documents show net, VAT, and gross separately (the arithmetic their procurement office reconciles: net ₦1,000-per-unit lines carrying ₦75 VAT to ₦1,075 gross). The posture check joins the pre-issue verification on both channels.</p><p><b>Step 4 — discounts assigned to instruments (Chapter 6).</b> The audit of existing practice moves each case to its instrument: the chain pricing that was being conceded per document moves to the Trade list where it belongs; the volume break stands as the registered Pricing Rule (module 2's ₦950-from-50-units); one-case concessions remain document concessions with ladder approvals — the Montaigne terminal concession the standing example. The additional discount field's permission narrows to the roles the matrix names.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the weekly habit installed (Chapter 7).</b> The five-check read runs its first weeks: the trend read explains a <b>30% movement across three periods</b> in one territory (a chain's new branches concentrating volume — the explanation written into the note); the sales register accumulates the period's split (standard-rated consumables, exempt pharmaceuticals) ready for the return; delivered-not-billed clears at each invoicing run. The weekly notes file; the first monthly commercial review works from four notes and the monthly reads, and its decisions — one list correction, one rule end-dated — enter the governed lanes.</p><p><b>The layer's evidence.</b> The configuration register holds the templates and their purposes; the ruling table and classification reads hold the treatments; the policy holds the postures; the discounts read holds the concession evidence; the weekly notes hold the reading habit. The tax and pricing layer is answerable end to end — from any document's tax row back to its template, rate, and account, and from any transacted price back to its instrument and approval.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in tax and pricing operation, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the hand-edited tax row.</b> Symptom: tax amounts adjusted on documents to match expectations; the return disagreeing with the configuration; rounding differences balanced by edits. Correction: the affected documents corrected through the proper lanes; the configuration question resolved at the template. Prevention: tax computed by configuration only, and template-write permission held per the matrix.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the misclassified line.</b> Symptom: an exempt item computing at 7.5% or a standard item at zero; the document corrected by hand so the next document repeats the error. Correction: the item's template fixed through the accountant's lane; affected documents corrected. Prevention: the refusal rule — classification questions resolve at the item record — and the two classification reads.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the mixed posture.</b> Symptom: inclusive rates entered on the exclusive channel or the reverse; prices 7.5% adrift or VAT under-declared. Correction: the affected list re-based arithmetically as a governed change. Prevention: one posture per channel by written policy, configured once, checked in the pre-issue read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the instrument misassigned.</b> Symptom: the same discount conceded per document monthly by hand; rules created for single negotiations; the additional discount field used to hide line pricing. Correction: each standing case moved to its instrument — list, rule, or approved concession. Prevention: the assignment rule and the monthly discounts read.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unread configuration.</b> Symptom: templates, treatments, and postures assumed correct because they were set correctly once; drift discovered at the return. Correction: the reads run and the findings fixed at their sources. Prevention: the weekly register pass, the monthly document sample, and the quarterly classification read.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the unexplained trend.</b> Symptom: analytics read but movements unexplained; the 30% shift noticed at quarter end with its cause cold. Correction: the investigation run late and the explanation recorded. Prevention: the trend read weekly with explanations written into the note.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the VAT context — the 7.5% rate, the liability flow, exempt classes, and the e-invoicing rules; build and read a template — row types, account heads, the standard set; operate item tax templates on mixed documents with the refusal rule; apply both pricing arithmetics and the one-posture-per-channel policy; assign every discount to its instrument with its holder and record; and run the fifteen-minute five-check read with the trend explanations.</p><p><b>Module rule 6: tax on a document is computed by configuration — the accountant's templates and treatments, selected not edited, at the channel's declared posture — and every price below list is one of three governed instruments with its record; the weekly reads confirm both before the invoice declares them.</b> Module 7 covers the Sales Invoice, payments, and collections.</p>"),
]

